<!-- NebulaBackground's own #bg-dim sits deliberately BELOW the ships/stars
     canvas (#sc) so it only darkens the nebula glow, not the animated
     foreground CRAFT already brightens — correct for the other pages, but
     this page is now dense, text-heavy tables/cards that need to read
     clearly over whatever's moving behind them. A second, page-local scrim
     ABOVE the whole nebula stack (not touching the shared component, so
     index/about/contact are unaffected) — plain fixed div, no explicit
     z-index needed: it paints above #window (position:fixed, z-index:auto)
     purely by coming later in the DOM at the same stacking tier, and stays
     below real content since every content section already carries an
     explicit z-index that beats z-index:auto regardless of DOM order.
     backdrop-filter:blur here softens the stars/ships themselves too, same
     "frosted glass" trick the featured hero's own .hero::after already
     uses over its title card — just extended across the whole page instead
     of one small area, since backdrop-filter only blurs what's behind an
     element, never the real content stacked above it. -->
<div id="content-scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>
<style>
  #content-scrim{position:fixed;inset:0;pointer-events:none;background:rgba(0,0,0,.5);
    backdrop-filter:blur(6px);-webkit-backdrop-filter:blur(6px)}
  /* backdrop-filter recomputes every frame the animated starfield behind it
     changes — one of the pricier CSS effects, worth skipping on phones and
     reduced-motion, matching the same LOW_POWER threshold nebula-engine.js
     already uses to cut its own animation work on these devices. The solid
     darkening (cheap, no compositing cost) still applies either way. */
  @media (--bp-900), (prefers-reduced-motion:reduce){
    #content-scrim{backdrop-filter:none;-webkit-backdrop-filter:none}
  }
